  "live_records": {
    "q-101957": {
      "dom_id": "step-q101957",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Given that $f(x)=2x+1$, what is ${f}^{′}(x)$ according to the definition of the derivative?",
      "worked_solution": "The derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)=2x+1$,  we have that $f(x+h)=2(x+h)+1.$ Hence,  we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{2(x+h)+1-(2x+1)}{h}).\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-101957-a",
              "type": "math",
              "value": "{f}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{(x+h)+x}{h})",
