    "q-47689": {
      "dom_id": "step-q47689",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given that $f(x)=2x$, the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ can be computed using the definition of the derivative. The first few steps of this calculation are shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2(x+h)-2x}{h} \\\\  & =…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinish the calculation, and hence find ${f}^{′}(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "By proceeding with the remaining calculations we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2(x+h)-2x}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2x+2h-2x}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{2h}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(x)=2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
